An integration test starts the real broker and database in containers, publishes each message type a consumer handles twice with the same id, restarts the consumer, publishes it a third time, and compares the state with a single delivery.

Tool: Testcontainers (Kafka, RabbitMQ or LocalStack module) with Vitest

```ts
import { runWith } from './harness'   // starts broker and database with Testcontainers, runs the real consumer
import { MESSAGE_SAMPLES } from '../src/messages/samples'   // one sample per message type

describe.each(MESSAGE_SAMPLES)('$type', (sample) => {
  it('has the effect of one delivery when delivered three times', async () => {
    const once = await runWith([sample])
    const thrice = await runWith([sample, sample], { restart: true, then: [sample] })
    expect(thrice.state).toEqual(once.state)
    expect(thrice.duplicatesCounted).toBe(2)
  })
})
```

A test for each consumer delivers every message it handles twice, including a redelivery after a simulated crash before the acknowledgement, and compares the effects with a single delivery.

Tool: Vitest with Testcontainers (PostgreSQL and the broker)

```ts
describe.each(consumers)('%s', (consumer) => {
  it('has the same effect for a message delivered twice', async () => {
    const message = consumer.sampleMessage()
    await consumer.handle(message)
    const once = await snapshotEffects()
    await consumer.handle(message)                       // redelivery
    expect(await snapshotEffects()).toEqual(once)
  })
})
```

An integration test saves a record through the element, reads the row directly from the database and fails if any of the sensitive fields holds the original value.

Tool: Vitest with Testcontainers (PostgreSQL)

```ts
it('stores sensitive fields as ciphertext', async () => {
  const card = '4111111111111111'
  const { id } = await customers.create({ name: 'Ada', cardNumber: card })
  const { rows } = await pg.query('select card_number from customers where id = $1', [id])
  expect(rows[0].card_number).not.toContain(card)
  expect((await customers.get(id)).cardNumber).toBe(card)
})
```

An integration test runs many concurrent operations that compete for the same records against the real database engine (not an in-memory fake), at the isolation level and with the retries of the decision, and checks the invariant afterwards.

Tool: Vitest with Testcontainers (PostgreSQL)

```ts
it('never sells more than is in stock', async () => {
  await db.query("update stock set available = 10 where sku = 'A'")
  const attempts = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, () => placeOrder({ sku: 'A', quantity: 1 }).then(() => 1, () => 0))
  const sold = (await Promise.all(attempts)).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)
  const { rows } = await db.query("select available from stock where sku = 'A'")
  expect(sold).toBe(10)
  expect(rows[0].available).toBe(0)
})
```

AWS Config managed rules evaluate every database, snapshot, volume and bucket continuously and report one that is not encrypted at rest. Azure Policy and Google Organization Policy have equivalent built-in rules.

Tool: AWS Config managed rules

```hcl
locals {
  encryption_rules = [
    "RDS_STORAGE_ENCRYPTED",
    "RDS_SNAPSHOT_ENCRYPTED",
    "ENCRYPTED_VOLUMES",
    "S3_BUCKET_SERVER_SIDE_ENCRYPTION_ENABLED",
  ]
}

resource "aws_config_config_rule" "encrypted_at_rest" {
  for_each = toset(local.encryption_rules)
  name     = lower(replace(each.value, "_", "-"))
  source {
    owner             = "AWS"
    source_identifier = each.value
  }
}
```

A database test run in CI after the migrations asserts the privileges of every role on each entity's tables: the owner writes, others at most read.

Tool: pgTAP (run with pg_prove)

```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT plan(3);
SELECT table_privs_are('public', 'orders', 'order_service', ARRAY['SELECT', 'INSERT', 'UPDATE', 'DELETE']);
SELECT table_privs_are('public', 'orders', 'inventory_service', ARRAY['SELECT'], 'inventory reads orders, never writes');
SELECT table_privs_are('public', 'orders', 'reporting', ARRAY['SELECT']);
SELECT * FROM finish();
ROLLBACK;
```

A dependency rule fails the build when code outside the outbox relay imports the broker's client, which would mean a message sent outside the transaction of its change.

Tool: dependency-cruiser

```js
// .dependency-cruiser.cjs
module.exports = {
  forbidden: [{
    name: 'publish-through-outbox',
    comment: 'ADR At-least-once delivery: messages go to the outbox table, only the relay talks to the broker',
    severity: 'error',
    from: { pathNot: '^src/outbox/relay' },
    to: { path: 'node_modules/(kafkajs|@confluentinc/kafka-javascript|amqplib|@aws-sdk/client-sqs|@aws-sdk/client-sns)' },
  }],
}
```

