An integration test against the real broker in a container publishes numbered messages for 100 keys interleaved, runs four consumers, and checks that each key's numbers were handled in increasing order.

Tool: Testcontainers (Kafka, RabbitMQ or LocalStack module) with Vitest

```ts
it('keeps the order within each key', async () => {
  const keys = Array.from({ length: 100 }, (_, i) => 'order-' + i)
  for (let n = 1; n <= 20; n++) for (const key of keys) await publish({ key, n })
  const handled = await consumeWith(4, keys.length * 20)          // [{ key, n }] in handling order
  for (const key of keys) {
    const ns = handled.filter((m) => m.key === key).map((m) => m.n)
    expect(ns).toEqual([...ns].sort((a, b) => a - b))
  }
})
```

An integration test delays replication on purpose (a paused WAL replay on the replica) and checks that a user's read straight after their write still returns it.

Tool: Vitest with Testcontainers (PostgreSQL primary and replica)

```ts
it('shows a user their own write while the replica lags', async () => {
  await replica.query('select pg_wal_replay_pause()')
  for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    await api.as(user).put('/me/address', { street: `Street ${i}` })
    const { body } = await api.as(user).get('/me/address')
    expect(body.street).toBe(`Street ${i}`)
  }
  await replica.query('select pg_wal_replay_resume()')
})
```

A test lists the operations from the router (not from a list kept by hand), calls each as a caller of every role or rule case, and compares allowed or refused with the expected matrix; it also calls each operation that takes an id with the id of a record of another user. An operation without an entry fails the test.

Tool: Vitest with supertest (or the test runner and HTTP client of your stack)

```ts
import request from 'supertest'
import { app, routes } from '../src/app'          // routes: the router's table
import { EXPECTED } from './authz/expected'       // "METHOD path" -> roles allowed
import { CASES, tokenFor, otherUsersRecord } from './authz/fixtures'

describe.each(routes)('$method $path', (route) => {
  it.each(CASES)('as %s gives the expected answer', async (who) => {
    const res = await request(app)[route.method.toLowerCase()](route.example)
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${await tokenFor(who)}`)
    const allowed = EXPECTED[`${route.method} ${route.path}`]?.includes(who) ?? false
    expect([403, 404].includes(res.status)).toBe(!allowed)
  })
  it.runIf(route.path.includes(':id'))('refuses another user\'s record', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)[route.method.toLowerCase()](route.path.replace(':id', await otherUsersRecord()))
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${await tokenFor('owner')}`)
    expect([403, 404]).toContain(res.status)
  })
})
```

An integration test signs in on two instances, revokes the session on one and polls the other until it refuses, failing past 60 seconds; a second test advances the clock past 15 idle minutes and expects the next request to be refused.

Tool: Vitest (fake timers) against two instances started by Testcontainers or docker compose

```ts
it('a revoked session stops on every instance in time', async () => {
  const token = await signIn(instanceA)
  await signOut(instanceA, token)
  const started = Date.now()
  while ((await call(instanceB, token)).status !== 401) {
    expect(Date.now() - started).toBeLessThan(REVOKE_SECONDS * 1000)
    await sleep(1000)
  }
})
```

An integration test against a local KMS writes a person's fields, copies the ciphertext as a backup would, erases the person and expects the key to be pending deletion and every decryption to fail.

Tool: Vitest with LocalStack (AWS KMS) or a Vault dev server

```ts
it('makes every copy of an erased person unreadable', async () => {
  const person = await createPerson({ email: 'shred@example.test' })
  const copies = [await store.rawFields(person.id), await events.rawFields(person.id)]  // ciphertext, as backups hold it
  await erasure.run(person.id)
  const key = await kms.send(new DescribeKeyCommand({ KeyId: person.keyId }))
  expect(key.KeyMetadata?.KeyState).toBe('PendingDeletion')
  for (const fields of copies) await expect(decryptFields(fields)).rejects.toThrow()
})
```

A test lists the element's operations from its router (not from a list kept by hand), calls each without a credential and with an expired one, and expects 401 from every one that is not on the public list. A new operation fails the test until it is protected or added to the public list, which shows up in the pull request.

Tool: Vitest with supertest (or the test runner and HTTP client of your stack)

```ts
import request from 'supertest'
import { app, routes } from '../src/app'          // routes: the router's table
import { PUBLIC } from '../src/auth/publicRoutes' // the public list, in the code

describe.each(routes.filter((r) => !PUBLIC.has(`${r.method} ${r.path}`)))('%s %s', (route) => {
  it('refuses a call without a credential', async () => {
    await request(app)[route.method.toLowerCase()](route.example).expect(401)
  })
  it('refuses an expired token', async () => {
    await request(app)[route.method.toLowerCase()](route.example).set('Authorization', `Bearer ${EXPIRED}`).expect(401)
  })
})
```
